The term `getQuizById` is a common naming convention for a function used in web development to retrieve specific quiz data from a data source, such as an API or a database[1][2].

It is not a built-in JavaScript method (like `document.getElementById`)[3][4], but rather a custom function you would typically implement in your application logic.

### Common Use Cases
*   **API Integration:** Fetching a specific quiz from a backend server by its unique identifier[1][2].
*   **Data Management:** Retrieving quiz details from a local array or object for display in a quiz application[5][6].

### Example Implementation (JavaScript/TypeScript)
Depending on your project structure, the implementation will vary. Below are two common patterns:

#### 1. Fetching from an API (Asynchronous)
If your quiz data is stored in a database, you would typically use the `fetch` API to retrieve it[1].

```javascript
async function getQuizById(quizId) {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(`https://api.example.com/quizzes/${quizId}`);
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error('Quiz not found');
    }
    const quiz = await response.json();
    return quiz;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error fetching quiz:', error);
  }
}
```

#### 2. Retrieving from Local Data
If you have an array of quizzes in your code, you can use the `.find()` method.

```javascript
const quizzes = [
  { id: 1, title: "JavaScript Basics" },
  { id: 2, title: "CSS Fundamentals" }
];

function getQuizById(id) {
  return quizzes.find(quiz => quiz.id === id);
}

// Usage
const myQuiz = getQuizById(1);
console.log(myQuiz.title); // Output: JavaScript Basics
```

### Important Note vs. `document.getElementById`
Do not confuse this with the native JavaScript function **`document.getElementById()`**[3][4].
*   **`document.getElementById('id')`**: Used to select an HTML element from the DOM to manipulate it (e.g., changing its text or style)[3].
*   **`getQuizById(id)`**: A custom function designed to handle data logic, not DOM selection[6].

